  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 240 PM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nOver the weekend and into next week, zonal flow will remain \nover the Northern CONUS until a low-pressure system from the \nnorthwest slides eastward on Monday, followed by a return to \nsouthwesterly zonal flow. On Saturday and Sunday, afternoon \nshowers are expected due to shortwave troughs and diurnal \nheating, though confidence remains low to medium (25\u201340%). On \nMonday, showers are expected to form in the afternoon as the \ncold front arrives. There is a 60% chance of showers, but the \nexact timing during the day remains uncertain due to variability\nin the fronts arrival.\n\nAfternoon temperatures will start in the low 80s on Saturday, \nthen rise into the low to mid-90s on Sunday. For the remainder \nof the period, temperatures are forecast to hover around 80\u00b0F. \nHowever, with the cold front passing through Monday, \ntemperatures may end up lower than expected; as the NBM 75th- \nto-25th percentile spread exceeds 10\u00b0F. There is medium \nconfidence (60%) in this cooler scenario, with potential \nafternoon highs in the upper 70s. \n\nCritical fire weather is not expected to develop on Sunday. \nHowever, wind gusts up to 30 knots and relative humidity dipping\ninto the low 20s within the Colorado counties will push \nGrassland Fire Danger Index (GFDI) values above 50. The primary \nlimiting factor is relative humidity, which would need to drop \nbelow 15% for critical conditions. Consequently, confidence that\nfire weather conditions will intensify further remains low \n(20%) due to the incoming cold front.",
